          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"flaubert-madame-bovary-fb5efdc59deab9a7\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":3,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Emma directly avows her maternal love for Berthe. The exclamative construction “Comme je t’aime!”, its immediate repetition, and its emergence during a sudden rush to embrace the child also give the words substantial expressive-discharge force. They do not undertake a commitment or obligation.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The E score is somewhat calibration-sensitive: the passage can also be read as an intensely delivered avowal, but the syntactic exclamation and narration of a sudden, excessive outpouring provide positive evidence beyond intensity alone.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Emma directly addresses her daughter Berthe; the highlighted occurrence is the second instance in the repeated exclamation “Comme je t’aime… comme je t’aime!”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"After her father’s letter evokes childhood happiness and makes her confront her present misery, Emma hears Berthe laughing and abruptly redirects intense affection toward her. She rushes to embrace, kiss, clean, and question the child while shedding tears. The servant’s astonishment at this “excès de tendresse” frames the episode as an unusually sudden outpouring, while the subsequent narrative suggests that this maternal revival may be unstable or compensatory; that instability does not negate the utterance’s avowal force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Comme je t’aime, ma pauvre enfant! comme je t’aime!”\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense declaration directly presents Emma’s love for Berthe as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Emma says the words while “se précipitant pour l’embrasser,” with an exclamative “Comme” construction and immediate repetition.\",\"supports\":\"The exclamative form and abrupt production during her rush toward Berthe support an expressive outpouring in addition to the avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.92},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She continues kissing Berthe, cries a little, and the servant is astonished by “cet excès de tendresse.”\",\"supports\":\"The narrator explicitly frames the episode as an excessive affective discharge, reinforcing E while contextualizing the avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The utterance follows Emma’s nostalgic response to her father’s letter and her hearing Berthe’s laughter.\",\"supports\":\"The emotional transition explains the sudden maternal outpouring and its potentially compensatory character.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as affirmative, present-tense, and a valid explicit first-person love expression.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the grammatical and extraction-level identification of the core love utterance.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the direct maternal avowal and E captures the independently evidenced exclamative outpouring. No commitment force or residual core function requires P or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
